      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1924 residents chose the name Mar Vista—Spanish for “ocean view”—to distinguish themselves from neighboring cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Mar_Vista,_Los_Angeles",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Venice Short Line railway, built in 1902 along present‑day Venice Boulevard, sparked Mar Vista’s early growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Mar_Vista,_Los_Angeles",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The 1924 opening of the Busby Building, the first commercial structure, housed the Chamber of Commerce and marked the start of civic organization.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Mar_Vista,_Los_Angeles",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mar Vista joined Los Angeles as the city’s 70th community in 1927, officially becoming part of the metropolis.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar_Vista%2C_Los_Angeles",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
